          "session_id": "full-mt-m-002",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My doctor says I have type 2 diabetes.",
          "answer": "A type 2 diabetes diagnosis means your blood sugar (glucose) stays above the normal range over time. In this condition, the body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, a hormone that helps move sugar out of the blood, and the pancreas cannot keep up with the extra demand. It develops gradually over years, and many people have no symptoms when it is found. (Source: *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThe passages I found do not fully describe the usual next steps after diagnosis. Your doctor is the right person to explain your own plan, including any tests, targets and treatment options. A useful question to ask is whether you should be referred for diabetes self-management education, which the diabetes adherence guidance describes as a core part of care, offered at diagnosis. (Source: *Sticking with a diabetes regimen*.)\n\nSome general lifestyle points from the guidance:\n\n- **Weight:** Where body weight is above the healthy range, even a sustained loss of about 5% can help blood sugar, blood pressure and cholesterol. Some people reach remission, meaning normal glucose without glucose-lowering medication, though monitoring continues. (Source: *Diet for type 2 diabetes*.)\n- **Activity:** Regular physical activity is part of the guidance. Nerve, eye or foot problems can change what kind of activity is safe, so check with your clinician first. (Source: *Physical activity for type 2 diabetes*.)\n- **Carbohydrates:** The guidance says \"sugar-free\" does not mean carbohydrate-free, and that carbohydrate targets should be set with your clinician. (Source: *Diet for type 2 diabetes*.)\n\nIf you have symptoms that worry you, such as extreme thirst, very frequent urination, confusion, vomiting or trouble breathing, contact a clinician promptly or seek urgent care.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
