          "question": "And what should I be eating?",
          "answer": "The guidance I found does not give a full meal plan for type 2 diabetes. It gives some practical points:\n\n- **Starch counts.** \"Sugar-free\" does not mean carbohydrate-free, because starch raises blood sugar about as much as sugar does. (*Diet for type 2 diabetes*)\n- **Fruit is allowed.** Whole fruit is part of every recommended pattern. (*Diet for type 2 diabetes*)\n- **Carbohydrate targets come from your clinician.** The guidance does not set a single target for everyone. (*Diet for type 2 diabetes*)\n- **Weight matters.** If your weight is above the healthy range, a sustained loss of about 5% can help blood sugar, blood pressure and cholesterol. (*Diet for type 2 diabetes*)\n\nA related document on blood pressure describes an eating pattern built on vegetables, fruit, whole grains, low-fat dairy, poultry, fish, beans, nuts and vegetable oils. It limits saturated fat, full-fat dairy, fatty meat, sweets and sugary drinks. That document is about blood pressure, not diabetes, so ask your clinician or a dietitian whether it fits your situation.\n\nSeveral details would change the advice:\n\n- **Your age and weight goals**\n- **Other conditions**, such as kidney disease, heart disease or high blood pressure\n- **Your medications**, since some can affect how food and blood sugar interact\n- **Any pregnancy or plans for pregnancy**\n- **Food preferences, cultural needs, budget or cooking access**\n- **Whether you have had diabetes education or a dietitian referral**\n\nIf you share some of these, I can give more specific guidance from the corpus. Your clinician or a registered dietitian can then turn it into a plan for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
